      <h1>Charge Acceptance Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Charge acceptance is <strong>how fast a battery can take charge</strong> — measured
        by the current a partially-discharged battery accepts at a controlled voltage, or the amp-hours it recovers in
        a set window. It matters most for <strong>start-stop and regenerative systems</strong>, where the battery must accept
        frequent fast recharges. A falling charge-acceptance number predicts early failure before capacity or CCA do.
      </div>

      <h2>How it is measured</h2>
      <p>
        A common method (under <strong>EN 50342</strong>) discharges the battery to a defined state, then applies a
        charge voltage and measures the <strong>current the battery accepts</strong> over a fixed interval — the charge
        acceptance is the charge (amp-hours) recovered in that window. A healthy battery accepts charge quickly; a
        battery with high internal resistance — from sulfation or corrosion — accepts little. The test is effectively
        a read on internal resistance through the charging path rather than the discharge path.
      </p>

      <h2>Charge acceptance by technology</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Charge acceptance (relative)</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Conventional flooded</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Plain lead plates, no charge-acceptance additive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EFB</td><td>Higher</td><td>Carbon on the negative plate improves charge acceptance</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AGM</td><td>Highest</td><td>Glass-mat construction lowers internal resistance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        This is the concrete reason start-stop systems specify EFB or AGM: the higher charge acceptance means the
        battery recovers fast enough between engine-off events to stay charged. A flooded battery in the same
        duty cycle accepts charge too slowly and progressively under-charges.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it matters for start-stop and regeneration</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Why charge acceptance is critical</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Start-stop</td><td>Battery is recharged at every stop; slow acceptance leaves it under-charged</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Regenerative braking</td><td>Captures brief high-current charging bursts; slow acceptance wastes them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conventional SLI</td><td>Less critical — one full charge per drive cycle</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What a low number means</h2>
      <p>
        Charge acceptance declines as a battery ages, for the same reasons CCA does — rising internal resistance from
        sulfation and corrosion. Because the decline shows on the charging side first, a low charge-acceptance
        reading is often the <strong>earliest</strong> warning of a failing start-stop battery, before capacity or CCA
        have visibly fallen.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> Charge acceptance is the battery property nobody quotes but every start-stop
        system depends on — and it is the earliest signal of aging because it reads the charging side, where
        resistance rises before the discharge side shows it.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> It measures the charging path, where aging shows up first. A buyer or technician who
        checks charge acceptance catches a failing start-stop battery before it strands the driver — and understands
        why EFB and AGM exist in the first place.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

